=== CONCLUSION ===
2 independent live attempts, 100 seconds apart, both resulted in 100%
HTTP 429 across all 5 domains on the very first request/page for every
domain - 0 of 1,000 requested reviews retrieved either time. This is not a
one-off blip: the rate limit did not clear in the 100s window, and every
single request across both attempts (10 requests total, 5 domains x 2
attempts) was rejected identically.

data/raw/openwebninja/results/ is empty - not a single successful response
was received in either attempt. Every data/raw/openwebninja/requests/
<domain>-page1.json file records {"ok": false, "status": 429, "attempts": 4}
- this is the actual final (attempt 2) request state; attempt 1's request
files were overwritten in place by attempt 2 since write_json() in
fetch_domain_reviews() unconditionally overwrites the request file on
every non-cached attempt (only the result file is gated by an
if res_file.exists() check, which never became true here).

Possible causes (not confirmed - OpenWeb Ninja has no diagnostic/quota
endpoint to check directly):
1. The account's actual per-second/per-minute rate limit is stricter than
   the 350ms (~2.8 req/sec) spacing REQUEST_SPACING_MS assumes safe -
   the module comment in scripts/openwebninja_benchmark.py claims this is
   "safely under every documented plan's rate limit," but that assumption
   is not holding for this account today.
2. A broader, possibly temporary, service-side rate-limit/throttle on
   OpenWeb Ninja's API unrelated to this specific key.
3. Monthly quota exhaustion surfaced as HTTP 429 instead of a
   quota-specific status/message - possible but NOT confirmed; the user's
   own dashboard check reported quota as "definitely very high (1000+)"
   before this run, which argues against this explanation, though monthly
   quota and per-second rate limit are two different things and a
   dashboard's headline "quota remaining" figure may not surface a
   separate rate-limit tier.
No further live attempts were made after attempt 2, to avoid additional
metered/possibly-billed requests against a condition that had already
failed to clear once.
